<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Dongye</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An early people of Korea's east coast, noted in Chinese records for clan territories, a sky-worship festival and fine bows and small horses.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dongye occupied the eastern coast of the peninsula, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangwon-province">Gangwon</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamgyong-province">Hamgyong</a>. After 108 BCE it came under the Han commandery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lintun-commandery">Lintun</a>, later folded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a>, and Chinese sources treat its people as kin of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> in language and custom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>There was no king; local chiefs ruled, and each clan's hills and rivers were bounded so that trespassers paid fines in slaves or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Marriage within the clan was forbidden. In the tenth month people held the mucheon festival, dancing in worship of heaven, and they revered the tiger. Dongye was known for its bows of sandalwood, sea-leopard skins and a breed of small horses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dongye fell under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> control during the 2nd to 4th centuries and was fully absorbed by about 400. Its southern areas later passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dongye.jpg" alt="Dongye" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Ye.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Ye, Ye, Tongye</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=dongye" aria-label="Dongye on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7795 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dongye lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8115 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8115 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day North Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=dongye">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
